MICHAELSON:	Case NZOE, June 3, 1970. Subject: Lieutenant James Roberts, callsign Cobra One. (PAUSE) Lieutenant, you've landed yourself in quite a mess. My name is Lieutenant Michaelson. I'm here to debrief you pending transfer to our custody.

ROBERTS:	(RESENTFUL) Not interested in talking to some goddamned desk jockey.

MICHAELSON:	I don't think you realise how much trouble you're in. If you don't want to find yourself facing a firing squad - or worse - I suggest you start co-operating. Tell me about Shaw, starting with your deployment on the moon.

ROBERTS:	(HEAVY SIGH) Fine. The Justice picked up a distress call from a research base on the moon and we deployed with the Jackson to clear it up. Some scientists had been captured by the commies and we were the only reinforcements Command could spare while they chased after pieces of ancient history. (SCOFFS) They had no idea what they were doing, the whole damn war fell apart the moment they started chasing those things.

MICHAELSON:	And when you landed?

ROBERTS:	(SPAT) We did our jobs. (SARCASTICALLY QUOTING AN OATH) We upheld American interests in space.